toin sodium is part of a broader class of drugs known as CYP enzyme inducers. While phenytoin itself is not explicitly labeled as a member of 'CYP强诱导剂' (CYP strong inducer) or 'CYP3A4诱导物' (CYP3A4 inducer) in the provided data, it is functionally grouped with such agents through shared mechanisms and co-occurrence in clinical contexts. For example, carbamazepine—a known CYP strong inducer—is discussed alongside phenytoin and phenobarbital as drugs that accelerate vitamin D metabolism. Furthermore, phenytoin’s impact on lamotrigine clearance aligns with the behavior of other CYP inducers. This places phenytoin sodium within a high-risk pharmacological cluster where drug–drug interactions are common and potentially severe. [Data: Entities (9786, 9714, 9792); Relationships (56054, 4148)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Significant drug–drug interactions involve phenytoin sodium and other medications\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Phenytoin sodium participates in multiple clinically relevant drug–drug interactions. It interacts with bilirubin (胆红素), though the exact clinical implication is less clear from the evidence provided, which primarily discusses pH-dependent absorption principles. More concretely, phenobarbital (苯巴比妥)—another antiepileptic—is shown to increase the clearance of verapamil hydrochloride (盐酸维拉帕米), illustrating how enzyme induction by one drug alters the pharmacokinetics of another. Given that both phenytoin and phenobarbital are enzyme inducers, their co-administration can amplify metabolic effects on co-prescribed drugs, leading to reduced efficacy or unexpected toxicity. These interactions emphasize the need for careful polypharmacy management in patients on antiepileptic regimens. [Data: Relationships (49548, 46009)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"The community includes connections to serious underlying diseases\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Several entities in this community are tied to severe m